      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs28 #72, 2026-08-11): a genuine PCRE
      ⍝ runtime error (e.g. the match limit being exceeded on a
      ⍝ pathological pattern) was reported to the caller exactly like an
      ⍝ ordinary "no match" -- an empty result, no way to tell the two
      ⍝ apart -- instead of raising DOMAIN ERROR with the libpcre error
      ⍝ text. Fixed in RegexpMatch::RegexpMatch() (Regexp.cc):
      ⍝ PCRE2_ERROR_NOMATCH is still silently ⍬ (that one really is "no
      ⍝ match", not an error), but any OTHER negative match_result now
      ⍝ raises DOMAIN ERROR instead of falling through to the same
      ⍝ empty-result path.
      ⍝
      '(a+)+$' ⎕RE 'aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaab'
DOMAIN ERROR+
      '(a+)+$' ⎕RE 'aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaab'
      ^        ^
      )MORE
libpcre error: match limit exceeded
      )SIC

      ⍝ control: a genuine (non-error) non-match is still silently ⍬
      ⍝
      'xyz' ⎕RE 'abc'

      )MORE
NO )MORE ERROR INFO

      ⍝ regression (Bugs28 #98): A ⎕RE['g'] B was quadratic in the
      ⍝ number of matches -- the pattern is compiled with PCRE2_UTF, so
      ⍝ pcre2_match() re-validated the UTF-32 encoding of the *entire*
      ⍝ subject on every single call unless told not to, on top of a
      ⍝ result-accumulation loop that also rebuilt/copied the growing
      ⍝ result on every doubling. Both fixed (RegexpMatch now passes
      ⍝ PCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK once the first call has already validated
      ⍝ the subject, and Quad_RE::regex_results() collects matches in a
      ⍝ plain std::vector instead); this checks correctness (result
      ⍝ count and content) over enough matches to exercise the
      ⍝ multi-match accumulation path, not timing.
      ⍝
      ≢'a' ⎕RE['g'] 500⍴'a'
500

      ∧/'a'=∊'a' ⎕RE['g'] 500⍴'a'
1

